{"notes": "This is clearly a 2021-2022 style regime: unemployment has collapsed from 6.1% to 3.7% over a year (12m change -2.4pp, still falling at the margin), while headline CPI is accelerating hard (8.57% y/y, 3m annualized headline at 10%, core 3m annualized 5.5%, both yoy_change_3m positive) and sitting at the top of its 12m range (yoy_range_position=1.00). Labor tightness and inflation are NOT in tension here — both point the same direction: an overheating economy demanding more near-term tightening. Labor is tight and tightening further (unrate at 12m low, sahm_gap positive but distance_from_natural negative meaning below-natural unemployment, i.e. tight labor). Inflation is hot and accelerating on every cut of the data — trend, momentum, and level all rising. This is the dominant signal for the front end: the reaction function should be pricing MORE tightening, not less.

Financial conditions have been easing steadily (NFCI at 52w high in range_position terms, all recent changes positive/loosening) — this removes any constraint on the Fed continuing to tighten, reinforcing the inflation/labor case rather than opposing it. Balance sheet is still expanding (assets at 52w high, positive 4w/13w/52w changes) — no QT here, so no offsetting restrictive impulse from that channel; if anything this is a dovish/accommodative input that argues the Fed has more work to do via rates.

