ent": "DGS3MO", "weight": -0.4}], "conviction": 0.45, "rationale": "Tight labor and sticky core inflation argue for the market to price slightly more near-term tightening risk concentrated at the 1y point versus 3mo, a modest front-end bear-flattener."}}

{"notes": "Labor: unemployment rate is essentially flat over 3/6/12m (3.5% now vs 3.5-3.6% range all year), sitting mid-range (0.50 position) and only 0.5pp below "natural" estimate. Sahm gap tiny (+0.17pp), nowhere near recession-signal territory. This is a stable, tight labor market — no clear loosening or tightening impulse from the print itself. I read this as flat/tight, not loosening, since the 3m uptick (+0.10pp) is noise within a 0.2pp 12m range.

Inflation: headline yoy has fallen sharply (3m change -1.66pp, now near 12m low at 3.29%, range position 0.04 — essentially at the bottom of its year range). Core PCE also declining (yoy_change_3m -0.49pp) and 3m annualized core (2.80%) is much cooler than the 12m yoy (4.30%), signaling disinflation momentum building even though level is still 2.3pp above target. Headline 3m annualized (2.35%) is close to target. This is a clear cooling trend, not a hot-inflation stall.

