ove."}], "trade": {"legs": [{"instrument": "DGS6MO", "weight": -0.5}, {"instrument": "DGS2", "weight": 0.5}], "conviction": 0.45, "rationale": "Express less near-term tightening via modest front-end bull-steepening: richer 6m as cuts get priced sooner, while 2y anchors the level view without reaching beyond front end."}}

{"notes": "Labor: unemployment has drifted up from 3.4% to 3.9% over 12 months, with the last two prints holding at 3.9% (range position at the top of its 12m band, Sahm gap +0.43pp — close to recession-signal territory). This is a clear loosening trend in the labor market's own measurement, even though slow and not yet confirmed by a fresh acceleration. Inflation: core PCE yoy has fallen steadily from 4.78% to 3.12% over the past year, a clear disinflation trend, though the last few prints have flattened (3.06→3.12) and 3m-annualized core/headline are both running hot near 4.75-4.78%, an uncomfortable re-acceleration signal that partly offsets the yoy trend. Headline yoy also ticked up in the last print (3.16→3.49). So inflation's own measurement is decelerating on a yoy basis but showing early signs of stalling/reversing on a monthly basis.

Resolving the tension: labor is loosening at the margin (unemployment up, sustained at cycle highs) while inflation's disinflation trend is losing momentum. The reaction function should weight the more persuasive, less noisy signal — the 12m labor deterioration is more unambiguous than the single-month inflation uptick. Net: modestly less near-term tightening priced, but not a strong easing call, since inflation has not fully rolled over and the monthly re-acceleration keeps the Fed cautious. Financial conditions are stable/marginally easing (NFCI near flat, slightly negative track), not tightening the constraint. Balance sheet continues to shrink steadily (QT ongoing), a modest independent tightening force on term premia but not the front-end policy-rate driver.

